## Why US Companies Hire Nearshore Developers in Canada

### 1. Your working hours, not a handoff

Most of Canada's major tech hubs share time zones with the US. Toronto, Ottawa, and Montreal run on Eastern time, and Vancouver runs on Pacific time. A Toronto-based developer overlaps fully with US East and Central teams and keeps about five hours of overlap with the West Coast. Stand-ups, code reviews, and production incidents happen in real time, with no overnight wait.

### 2. A deep and growing talent pool

Canadian cities rank among North America's top tech talent markets. In CBRE's *Scoring Tech Talent 2025* report, **Toronto moved up to 3rd** among North American markets, Vancouver ranked 10th, Ottawa 11th, and Montreal 15th. Toronto added **42,900 tech jobs between 2021 and 2024**. Tech talent jobs in Canada grew **5.9% in 2024, against 1.1% in the US**, and Toronto, Vancouver, and Montreal together hold **62% of Canada's AI talent**.

### 3. Familiar ways of working

Canadian engineers usually work with the same tools, cloud platforms, agile rituals, and security expectations as US teams. That shortens onboarding and cuts down on miscommunication.

### 4. Bilingual English and French talent

Canada is one of the few places where you can hire developers who build and test in both English and French. According to Statistics Canada's 2021 Census, **18.0% of Canadians (about 6.6 million people)** can hold a conversation in both languages, rising to **46.4% in Quebec**. This matters for any product serving Quebec, French-speaking customers, or multilingual markets.

